{{also|TOSH|Tosh}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/tɒʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-tosh.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒʃ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From 19th-century British thieves' cant, of uncertain origin. Sense of [[nonsense]] possibly influenced by {{m|en|tush||[[nonsense]]! [[tsk tsk]]!}} attested from 15th century.

====Alternative forms====
* {{lb|en|nonsense}} [[tush]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|Britain|slang|obsolete}} [[copper#Noun|Copper]]; [[item]]s [[make#Verb|made]] of copper.
#* {{RQ:Mayhew London Labour|volume=II|chapter=Of the Sewer-hunters|page=150|column=2|passage=The sewer-hunters were formerly, and indeed are still, called by the name of "Toshers," the articles which they pick up in the course of their wanderings along shore being known among themselves by the general term "'''tosh'''," a word more particularly applied by them to anything made of copper.|brackets=on}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|chiefly|Britain|slang|rare}} [[valuable#Noun|Valuables]] [[retrieve]]d from [[drain#Noun|drains]] and [[sewer]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1974|author={{w|Joan Aiken}}|title=Midnight is a Place|page=164
|passage=I am present engaged in fishing for '''tosh''' in the sewers of Blastburn.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|British|slang|uncountable}} [[rubbish|Rubbish]], [[trash]], {{lb|en|now especially}} [[nonsense]], [[bosh]], [[balderdash]]
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 26, 1892|work=Oxford University Magazine|number=26/1|passage=To think what I've gone through to hear that man! Frightful '''tosh''' it'll be, too.}}
#* {{RQ:Wells New Machiavelli|chapter=5|passage=Perhaps it helped a man into Parliament, Parliament still being a confused retrogressive corner in the world where lawyers and suchlike sheltered themselves from the onslaughts of common-sense behind a fog of Latin and Greek and twaddle and '''tosh'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Rowling Harry Potter|book=1|chapter=4|passage=‘Took yeh from the ruined house myself, on Dumbledore’s orders. Brought yeh ter this lot...’ ‘Load of old '''tosh''',’ said Uncle Vernon.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|archaic|_|school slang|countable}} A [[bath]] or [[foot]] [[pan]]
#* '''1881''', Leathes in C.E. Pascoe, ''Everyday Life in our Public Schools'', ii. 20
#*: A ‘'''tosh'''’ pan... is also provided.
#* '''1905''', H. A. Vachell, ''Hill'', i
#*: We call a tub a '''tosh'''.
# {{lb|en|cricket|slang|disparaging|uncountable}} [[easy|Easy]] [[bowling]]
#* '''1898 June 25''', ''Tit-Bits'', 252/3
#*: Among the recent neologisms of the cricket field is ‘'''tosh'''’, which means bowling of contemptible easiness.
# {{lb|en|UK|jocular|_|slang|uncountable}} ''Used as a form of address''.
#* '''1954''', E. Hyams, ''Stories & Cream'', 175
#*: [['ere|'Ere]], '''tosh''', you [[been|bin]] at [[Chatham|Cha'ham]]?

